### Option 1: Independent contractors

You sign a services contract with the developer, who invoices you, usually monthly.

**Pros:** fast, flexible, no local entity needed.

**Cons:**

- **Misclassification risk.** If a contractor works full time, only for you, under your direction, with your equipment, they may legally be an employee. That can create liabilities for back benefits and taxes.
- **No statutory benefits**, such as provident fund contributions and gratuity, which can make you less attractive to candidates who want stable employment.
- **Intellectual property**: make sure the contract clearly assigns IP to your company.

Contractors are a good fit for defined projects, part-time work and people who genuinely run their own freelance business.

### Option 2: Employer of Record (EOR)

An EOR is a company with an Indian entity that becomes the legal employer of your developer. It runs payroll, deducts taxes, provides statutory benefits and handles compliance. You manage the person's daily work.

**Pros:** compliant, quick to set up, offers proper employment to candidates.

**Cons:** a monthly fee per employee, and less control over contracts and benefit details.

An EOR is often the best choice for a startup's first one to ten hires in India.

### Option 3: Your own entity

Registering a private limited company in India gives you full control, but involves incorporation, local directors, accounting, payroll, tax filings and compliance. It usually makes sense once you have a sizeable team or plan a long-term office.

## Salary and compensation in India

- **Post salaries in rupees**, in lakhs per annum (LPA), which is how candidates compare offers. For example, "₹30–40 LPA".
- **CTC (cost to company)** is the common way offers are quoted. It often includes base salary, employer provident fund contributions, gratuity and bonuses. Be clear about what's fixed and what's variable.
- **Equity**: ESOPs are well understood in India's startup scene. Explain your vesting and exercise terms clearly.
- **Benefits candidates expect**: health insurance (often including family), paid leave, and equipment.
- **Pay varies a lot** by city, company type and seniority. Global companies hiring remotely often pay above local averages to attract senior talent.

## Notice periods: plan for them

Notice periods in India are often 30, 60 or 90 days, and senior engineers frequently have 60 to 90. This affects your timeline:

- Ask about the notice period early in the process.
- Some companies offer to **buy out** part of the notice period.
- Expect that some candidates will receive counter-offers from their current employer during the notice period. Keep in touch regularly between offer and joining day.

On GetHired, notice period is a standard field you can add to any application form, with an optional rule to flag long notice periods for review. See [knockout questions](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/knockout-questions-how-to-screen-out-unqualified-applicants-without-losing-good-ones).

## Working across time zones

India Standard Time (IST) is UTC+5:30 and doesn't use daylight saving.

- **Europe:** good afternoon overlap.
- **US East Coast:** a short overlap in the Indian evening and US morning.
- **US West Coast:** little natural overlap; needs planning and strong async habits.

Set expectations in the job post: "Remote in India, with at least 3 hours of overlap with 9:00–12:00 ET." Don't expect late-night meetings every day; it's the fastest way to lose good people.

## How to find and screen developers in India

1. **Write a clear job post** with the salary in INR, the work model and overlap hours. Our [job description template](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/how-to-write-a-software-engineer-job-description-that-attracts-the-right-applicants-template) works well.
2. **Use platforms where Indian engineers search for remote roles.** GetHired's job seekers include many developers in India looking for remote work; read [how they search](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/remote-developer-jobs-in-india-how-to-find-apply-and-get-hired-2026-guide) to make your post stand out.
3. **Screen for async communication.** Ask a written question in the application, such as "Explain a technical decision you made recently to a non-technical teammate."
4. **Use a practical assessment.** See [take-home vs live coding vs pair programming](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/take-home-assignment-vs-live-coding-vs-pair-programming-choosing-a-technical-assessment).
5. **Move quickly.** Strong engineers often have several processes at once.

